-The seven $E_2$ conditions are affine-linear in $(s_1,s_2)$; a solution
-needs all $\binom{7}{3} = 35$ minors of the $7 \times 3$ matrix
-$[b(t) \mid M(t) \mid L(t)]$ to vanish.  Each minor is a homogeneous
-binary quintic in $(t_1,t_2)$; the $35 \times 6$ coefficient matrix has
-rank 6 over $K_5$, so the minors span all binary quintics and their
-only common zero in $\overline{K_5}^2$ is $(0,0)$.
-
-**What the Lean proof does:**
-- `hspan` is exactly the rank-6 claim: each of the 6 monomials
-  $t_1^{5-k}t_2^k$ ($k = 0,\dots,5$) is a $K$-linear combination of the
-  35 minors.  (The script proves the stronger matrix-rank statement;
-  the Lean proof consumes its consequence.)
-- `hzero`: every minor vanishes at the true $(t_1,t_2)$ — this is
-  equation `eq:minors` evaluated at a putative solution.
-- `eval_eq`: evaluating a spanning relation at $t$ kills every minor,
-  so each monomial value $(t_0)^{5-k}(t_1)^k = 0$.
-- Specialising to $k = 0$ gives $t_1^5 = 0$; to $k = 5$ gives
-  $t_2^5 = 0$; `pow_eq_zero_iff` (field, no zero divisors) yields
-  $t_1 = t_2 = 0$, i.e. `t = 0`.
